<%# User instructions for electronic records (also an appendix of the procedures document). %>
<div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Writing an entry</h3>
  <p>On the aircraft's Logbook tab, choose <strong>+ New Entry</strong>. Give the date, the logbook (aircraft, engine, propeller), the airframe total time, and what was done; for an engine or propeller entry pick the component (its times fill in) and tick any sub-components. A template can start the wording. An entry isn't a signed record until it's signed.</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Signing</h3>
  <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5">
    <li>Add your certificate (kind and number) once, on your profile.</li>
    <li>Open the entry and choose <strong>Sign</strong>. Read the entry as shown: your signature covers exactly that.</li>
    <li>Pick the certificate you're signing with. The statement you'll certify is shown with it (return to service, or the inspection statement).</li>
    <li>Re-enter your password, or sign in again with single sign-on, tick the confirmation and choose <strong>Sign entry</strong>. You'll see a confirmation and get an email.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Never share your password or let someone else sign while you're signed in: a signature is yours alone. If you think someone has, tell the administrator straight away.</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Mistakes</h3>
  <p>A signed entry can't be edited or deleted. Open it and choose <strong>Correct this entry</strong>: a new entry starts from the original; fix it, save, and sign it. The original stays, marked “Superseded by a correction”.</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Someone else did the work</h3>
  <p>The owner can choose <strong>Ask someone to sign</strong> on the entry: the mechanic gets a one-time email link, signs in or creates their own account, adds their certificate and signs. If they prefer paper, print the entry (<strong>Print for logbook</strong>), have it signed in ink, and keep the paper with the logbooks (scan it into Documents).</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold">Checking a record</h3>
  <p>A signed entry shows who certified it, with which certificate, when (UTC), what they certified, and its status. <strong>Valid</strong> means nothing has changed since signing. The fingerprint (a SHA-256 of the entry) is printed on paper copies so a copy can be matched to the record. Every change to any record is kept under <strong>Activity → Record history</strong>.</p>
  <h3 class="font-semibold">If the system is down</h3>
  <p>Make the entry on paper and sign it in ink. When the system is back, add the entry and scan the signed paper into Documents; the paper itself stays with the logbooks and remains the signed record for that entry.</p>
</div>
